-- A second citation slot, for the energetics claim specifically.
--
-- ── WHY ONE CITATION SLOT WAS NEVER ENOUGH ─────────────────────────────────
-- An audit of all 201 excerpt-less citations found that of 112 PERFECTLY
-- verified excerpts, 65 (58%) do not support a temperature at all, and 50 of
-- those cite King's American Dispensatory. King's is an Eclectic materia medica:
-- it classifies by ACTION and assigns no thermal quality anywhere. The quotation
-- was genuine every time; it simply answered a different question.
--
-- That is the case for two slots. primary_text_citation keeps carrying identity,
-- indications and safety, which is what those sources genuinely support.
-- energetics_citation carries the temperature and taste claim, sourced to a work
-- that actually makes one.
--
-- ── WHAT IS LOADED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- 127 herbs had a temperature with no source that could carry it. A hunt
-- proposed pre-1900 sources for 97 and established that 30 have NONE anywhere,
-- almost all American and New World plants whose temperature is therefore modern
-- energetic inference and always will be.
--
-- The 97 were then re-read by FOURTEEN INDEPENDENT VERIFIERS in isolated
-- directories: 89 confirmed as a real temperature, 3 rejected as an action word
-- rather than a thermal, 3 wrong species or part, 2 with a locator that does not
-- resolve. An 8% loss, the best rate any pass on this project has produced.
--
-- Compared against production afterwards, since the hunters never saw the stored
-- values:
--   61  AGREE with the stored pole   -> loaded as provenance
--    8  DISAGREE                     -> loaded as a NOTE, value NOT changed
--   20  compound assignments         -> held, need reading individually
--
-- ── THE 8 DISAGREEMENTS ARE RECORDED, NOT APPLIED ──────────────────────────
-- Standing rule: classical sources corroborate, disambiguate or add. They never
-- override a clear modern value. Six of the eight are the Galenic-versus-modern
-- split documented throughout this project, where classical Western sources call
-- aromatic and alterative herbs hot and modern energetics calls them cool. That
-- is a category difference, not an error. No stored temperature changes here.
--
-- ── THE FELT-HEAT RULE, NOW SETTLED ────────────────────────────────────────
-- Founder ruling 2026-07-22, STRICT: a thermal must be stated as a property of
-- the DRUG in a thermal slot. A Galenic degree, 寒/涼/平/溫/熱, ushna or sita.
-- An adjective attached to an action word does NOT count, so Rafinesque's
-- "a warm stimulant" for Stone Root does not qualify and Stone Root stays
-- unsourced. This question had been answered both ways by different passes; the
-- strict reading is consistent with how the American Eclectic finding was
-- reached in the first place.
--
-- ── THE TIER FIELD MATTERS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- 23 of the 89 are second-hand: Dymock, Dutt, Watt and Ainslie are English
-- compilers reporting the Sanskrit nighantus, not the classical texts. Each is
-- marked tier = second-hand-report. Shatavari is the sharp case: Dymock writes
-- that the plants "appear to be" the Satavari of the Nighantas, so even the
-- identification is his inference, and his thermal covers two Asparagus species
-- jointly rather than the row's alone.
--
-- Idempotent.

begin;

alter table public.herbs
  add column if not exists energetics_citation jsonb;

comment on column public.herbs.energetics_citation is
  'Citation for the TEMPERATURE and TASTE claim specifically, kept apart from primary_text_citation because one source rarely supports both. Of 112 verified excerpts on this table, 58% supported the identity or indications but assigned no temperature at all, 50 of them citing King''s American Dispensatory, which is Eclectic and classifies by action. Keys: source, year (1899 or earlier), excerpt (verbatim), locator, assigns (the thermal in the source''s own words), corpus, tier (first-hand or second-hand-report), agreement (whether it agrees with the stored value). WHERE IT DISAGREES THE STORED VALUE IS NOT CHANGED: classical sources corroborate, disambiguate or add, never override. A thermal must be stated as a property of the drug in a thermal slot; an adjective on an action word does not qualify.';

-- ── 61 agreeing sources: provenance for a value already held ────────────────
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Dymock, W., Warden, C. J. H. & Hooper, D., Pharmacographia Indica: A History of the Principal Drugs of Vegetable Origin, Met With in British India, Vol. II (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner; Bombay: Education Society''s Press; Calcutta: Thacker, Spink)", "year": "1891", "excerpt": "It is described in the Nighantas as tonic, alterative, pungent, astringent, hot and aphrodisiac, and is recommended in rheumatism, cough, dropsy, consumption and senile debility.", "locator": "Vol. II, p. 566, order SOLANACEAE, entry heading \"WITHANIA SOMNIFERA, Dunal.\", opening of the \"History, Uses, &c.\" paragraph. Verified in the Wellcome scan, archive.org identifier b21960343_0002, file b21960343_0002_djvu.txt (title page reads VOL. II ... 1891).", "assigns": "HOT. Dymock''s word is \"hot\" (i.e. ushna virya), given as an explicit report of what the Nighantas assign. This is a genuine thermal quality, not an action word. NOTE ON MATCH: the stored value was not supplied to me in herbs.md, so I cannot state a match; if the row stores ''Warm'' the direction agree", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H001';  -- Ashwagandha (Withania somnifer
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Pharmacographia Indica: A History of the Principal Drugs of Vegetable Origin met with in British India, by William Dymock, C. J. H. Warden and David Hooper — Vol. III, Part VI", "year": "1893", "excerpt": "These two plants appear to be the Satávari and Maha-satávari of the Nighantás: among the synonyms of the first, we find Dvipika, Dvipa-satru, Vara-ghantika, Narayani, and Sata-padi; the synonyms of the second are very similar... Both plants are considered to be heavy and cold, sweet, demulcent, galactogogue, tonic, and strengthening, and to remove bilious and rheumatic humors, blood diseases, and swellings; they are used both internally and in the preparation of several medicated oils.", "locator": "Vol. III (Part VI, 1893), p. 483, entry headed ASPARAGUS RACEMOSUS, Willd. / ASPARAGUS SARMENTOSUS, Willd. (Liliaceae), section ''History, Uses, &c.'' Full-text OCR: https://archive.org/download/pharmacographia03dymogoog/pharmacographia03dymogoog_djvu.txt (title page confirms Vol. III, Part VI, London/Bombay/Calcutta, 1893)", "assigns": "''cold'' (i.e. sita virya), coupled with ''heavy'' (guru) and the sweet taste. MATCHES the stored value ''Cool'' in kind; the source''s word is the stronger ''cold'' rather than ''cool'', which is a wording difference the founder may want to note but not a contradiction. The ''Sweet'' half of the stored taste is", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H103';  -- Shatavari (Asparagus racemosus
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Li Shizhen 李時珍, Bencao Gangmu 本草綱目, juan 14, Herb section, entry 莎草香附子 (shacao xiangfuzi), the 氣味 (qi and flavour) head line", "year": "1596", "excerpt": "氣味甘微寒無毒 …… 宗奭曰苦　頌曰天寳單方云辛微寒無毒性濇　元素曰甘苦微寒氣厚於味　時珍曰辛微苦甘平", "locator": "本草綱目 (四庫全書本) 卷十四, 草部, entry 莎草香附子 {{别錄中品}}, the 氣味 head line. Verified by curl against the Wikisource raw text: https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/卷14&action=raw (184,742 bytes, full text not a table of contents). The entry anchor is at file line 245 and the 氣味 line at line 249. I trimmed the tail of Li Shizhen''s gloss where it continues into channel doctrine (足厥陰手少陽藥也 …… 兼行十", "assigns": "微寒, slightly cold. This MATCHES the stored ''Cool'' in direction. But it CONTRADICTS the parenthetical the row carries in the same field, that ''the Chinese tradition instead classes it as neutral'': the Bencao Gangmu head line is 微寒, and 平 (neutral) is Li Shizhen''s own dissenting gloss further down the", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H153';  -- Musta / Nut Grass / Xiang Fu (
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Gerard, John (rev. Thomas Johnson), The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes, London, 1633 — Chap. 68, \"Of the Bay or Laurel Tree\"", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "The Temperature and Virtues.\n\nA. The berries and leaves of the Bay tree, saith Galen, are hot and very dry, and yet the berries more than the leaves.\n\nB. The bark is not biting and hot, but more bitter, and it hath also a certain astrictive or binding quality.", "locator": "Gerard 1633, Book 3, Chap. 68 \"Of the Bay or Laurel Tree\", section \"The Temperature and Virtues\", paragraphs A and B. Verified in the Ex-Classics full transcription of the 1633 Johnson edition, https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt, lines 74630-74637.", "assigns": "HOT and very DRY, with an explicit gradation inside the plant: berries hotter than leaves, and the bark less hot but more bitter and binding. Corroborated by Culpeper, The Complete Herbal / London Dispensatory (1653), in the appended Catalogue of Simples under \"Herbs and their Leaves\": \"Laurus. Bay-", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H196';  -- Bay Laurel / Sweet Bay (Laurus
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "William Dymock, C. J. H. Warden and David Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica, Vol. III (reporting the Nighantas)", "year": "1890", "excerpt": "This plant is called by Sanskrit medical writers Punar-nava, Punar-bhava, and Punar-bhu, on account of its perennial habit, and Sothagni from its use as a remedy for dropsy. It is described in the Nighantas as pungent, dry, hot, sweet and bitter, and is recommended as a laxative, diuretic, and stomachic in jaundice, strangury, dropsy, and internal inflammations.", "locator": "Pharmacographia Indica, Vol. III, Nyctagineae, ''Boerhaavia repens, Linn.'', p. 130 (the entry opens after the page marker 129 and the quoted sentence precedes the marker 131). Verified in archive.org b21960343_0003_djvu.txt, lines 7579-7596; cross-checked against mobot31753000263381_djvu.txt line 6880, whose OCR breaks the same sentence mid-word and whose running heads mis-read 132 as 182.", "assigns": "Hot (with dry, and the rasas pungent, sweet, bitter). MATCHES the row''s stored ''Warm'', and the ''dry'' MATCHES the stored ''Dry''. The prior audit note already anticipated this passage; this result supplies the verbatim text, the volume and the page so it can be cited.", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H219';  -- Punarnava / Spreading Hogweed 
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "李時珍 Li Shizhen, 本草綱目 Bencao Gangmu, 卷三十四 木部・香木類, 桂 (identified in the text as 肉桂), 氣味 line", "year": "1596", "excerpt": "桂〔别録。時珍曰：此即肉桂也，厚而辛烈，去粗皮用，其去内外皮者即為桂心〕氣味甘辛大熱有小毒〔權曰桂心苦辛無毒。元素曰肉桂氣熱味大辛純陽也。杲曰桂辛熱有毒…〕", "locator": "本草綱目 (四庫全書本), 卷三十四, 木部・香木類, entry 桂 (別錄上品) / 牡桂 (本經上品); the 氣味 line for 桂 = 肉桂. Text: https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/卷三十四&action=raw (122 KB, full text, retrieved 2026-07-22). Bracketed material is Li Shizhen''s interlinear note, marked here as such and not run together with the main line.", "assigns": "大熱 (da re) = GREATLY HOT, with 小毒 (slight toxicity). This MATCHES the stored value Hot, and the 小毒 additionally supports the row''s toxicity note. Three further pre-1900 corroborations, all consistent: 元素 (Zhang Yuansu), quoted in the same entry, 「肉桂氣熱味大辛純陽也」; 宗奭 in the 發明 section, 「桂甘辛大熱」; and 汪昂, 本", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H201';  -- Cassia / Chinese Cinnamon, Rou
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Gerard, John (rev. Thomas Johnson), The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "The Temperature.\n\nThe seed of garden Cumin, as Galen saith, is hot and dry in the third degree: Dioscorides saith that it hath in it also a binding quality.", "locator": "The Second Book of the History of Plants, CHAP. 432, \"Of Cumin\", section headed \"The Temperature\" (immediately after \"The Names\" and before \"The Virtues\"). Verified against the full text at https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt, downloaded whole and grepped.", "assigns": "HOT AND DRY IN THE THIRD DEGREE. A full Galenic degree assignment covering BOTH temperature and moisture, plus \"a binding quality\" (astringency). NOTE ON MATCH: the stored value was not supplied to me. If the row stores ''Warm'', the direction agrees but degree three is materially hotter than a bare ''", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H121';  -- Cumin (Jiraka) — Cuminum cymin
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard / Thomas Johnson, The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes (Johnson''s revised and enlarged edition)", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "The Temperature. Cloves are hot and dry in the third degree. The Virtues. A. Cloves strengthen the stomach, liver, and heart, help digestion, and provoke urine.", "locator": "The Third Book, Chap. 150, ''Of the Clove Tree'', the ''The Temperature'' heading (immediately following ''The Names'' and preceding ''The Virtues''). Text file used: https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt (Ex-Classics transcription of the 1633 Johnson edition), lines 82622-82700.", "assigns": "Hot and dry in the third degree. MATCHES the stored value ''Hot''. Corroborated independently by Culpeper''s Catalogue of Simples, FRUITS section, degree lists: hot ''In the third degree. Juniper Berries, Cloves, Carpobalsamum, Cubebs, Anacardium, bitter Almonds'' and dry ''In the third. Cloves, Galls, &c", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H124';  -- Clove (Lavanga) — Caryophyllus
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal, appended ''The English Physician and Family Dispensatory'', catalogue of simples, section ''Of the Gums and Rosins''", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "Gums are either temperate, as, Lacca, Elemi, Tragacanth, &c. Intemperate, and so are hot in the first degree, as Bdellium, Gum of Ivy. In the second, Galbanum, Myrrh, Mastich, Frankincense, Olibanum, Pitch, Rozin, Styrax. In the third. Amoniacum. In the fourth. Euphorbium. Gum Arabick is cold.", "locator": "Culpeper 1653, appended dispensatory, the section following ''The College tells you a tale that there are such things in Rerum Natura, as these, Gums, Rozins, Balsams, and Juices made thick'', subsection headed ''2. Of the Gums and Rosins''. Verified in the full Project Gutenberg text of ebook 49513 (downloaded by curl); the gums-degree list runs at file lines 19396-19404, with ''Frankincense, Olibanum", "assigns": "Hot in the second degree. MATCHES the stored ''Warm''. It also puts a hard pre-1900 citation under the clause the row currently asserts without one (''consistent with the Unani reading of kundur as heating''). Note the source gives heat only; it assigns no moisture degree to olibanum.", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H191';  -- Frankincense (Olibanum) — Bosw
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Culpeper, Nicholas, The Complete Herbal / The English Physician, with the appended translation of the London Dispensatory, 1653 — Catalogue of Simples, \"Roots\", entry \"Rhaphani, Domesticae and Sylvestris\"", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "Rhaphani, Domesticae and Sylvestris. Of Raddishes, garden and wild. Garden Raddishes provoke urine, break the stone, and purge by urine exceedingly, yet breed very bad blood, are offensive to the stomach, and hard of digestion, hot and dry in quality. Wild, or Horse Raddishes, such as grow in ditches, are hotter and drier than the former, and more effectual.", "locator": "Culpeper 1653, appended Catalogue of Simples, \"Roots\" section, entry \"Rhaphani, Domesticae and Sylvestris\". Verified in Project Gutenberg ebook 49513, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt, lines 15799-15804. The plant identification is fixed by Culpeper''s own main entry, headed \"RADDISH, OR HORSE-RADDISH\" (same text, lines 10452-10470), which describes the horseradish root as \"gr", "assigns": "HOT and dry, at a degree ABOVE garden radish: garden radish is \"hot and dry in quality\", horseradish \"hotter and drier than the former\". No numbered Galenic degree is given. The herbs.md brief states the row''s stored temperature is Hot and its moisture Dry, so on the brief''s own account this MATCHES", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H217';  -- Horseradish (Armoracia rustica
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal, appended ''The English Physician and Family Dispensatory'', catalogue of simples, section ''HERBS AND THEIR LEAVES'', s.v. Satureia", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "Satureia. Savory. Summer savory is hot and dry in the third degree, Winter savory is not so hot, both of them expel wind.", "locator": "Culpeper 1653, appended dispensatory, alphabetical catalogue of simples, section ''HERBS AND THEIR LEAVES'' (section heading at file line 16128), headword ''Satureia'' at file lines 17245-17246 of the Project Gutenberg text of ebook 49513 (https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt), downloaded by curl. This is a self-contained one-plant paragraph between ''_Saponaria._'' and ''_Sazifragia al", "assigns": "Hot and dry in the third degree. MATCHES the stored ''Warm'' and the stored ''Dry''. BUT IT DIFFERS FROM THE DEGREE THE ROW ITSELF ASSERTS. The temperature field currently says ''Culpeper places it under Mercury, hot and dry in the second degree''. Culpeper''s text says THIRD degree, not second. Separately", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H234';  -- Summer Savory (Satureja horten
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "William Dymock, C. J. H. Warden and David Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica, Vol. III (reporting the Nighantas)", "year": "1890", "excerpt": "This well-known drug is the Kutaki of Sanskrit writers ... In the Nighantas it bears the following synonyms: Rohini, Katu-rohini, Vakragra, Matsya-pitta, Matsya-vinna, Kanda-ruha, Krishna-bhedi, Dvijangika, Asoka-rohini, Sakuladani and Chakranga. It is described as digestive, bitter, pungent, dry, aperient, light and cold; and is recommended as a remedy for worms, asthma, bile, phlegm, and fever.", "locator": "Pharmacographia Indica, Vol. III, Scrophularineae, ''Picrorhiza kurrooa, Benth.'', p. 10 (entry opens directly under the page marker ''10 SCROPHULARINEAE''; the entry runs on to p. 11). Verified in archive.org mobot31753000263381_djvu.txt, lines 699-724.", "assigns": "Cold (with dry, bitter and pungent). MATCHES the row''s stored Cold, and the same sentence also matches the stored bitter, katu/pungent and dry — all four stored energetics values rest on this one sentence.", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H289';  -- Kutki / Katuka (Picrorhiza kur
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "William Dymock, C. J. H. Warden and David Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica: A History of the Principal Drugs of Vegetable Origin met with in British India, vol. II, Gentianaceae, Swertia Chirata, reporting the Makhzan-el-Adwiya on Hindu practice", "year": "1890", "excerpt": "The author of the Makhzan-el Adwiya gives at the end of his article upon Kasab-ed-darira the following short summary of the manner in which Chiretta is used by the Hindu physicians:— \"They consider it to be cold and dry, light and flatulent, a remedy for colds and bilious affections, burning of the body, and the fever arising from derangement of the three humors which they call sannipat (fever with delirium).\"", "locator": "Pharmacographia Indica, vol. II, p. 512 (entry Swertia Chirata runs pp. 511-513; the volume index confirms \"Swertia chirata ... 511\"). Read from archive.org mobot31753000263373_djvu.txt (lines 25951-26000) and independently cross-verified word for word in a second, unrelated scan, archive.org b2129737x_0002_djvu.txt (lines 32735-32745). The two OCRs agree on the thermal phrase; the second scan is ", "assigns": "COLD AND DRY. This MATCHES the stored temperature Cold and, unusually, also matches the stored moisture Dry - both from one sentence. LABELLING, as the brief requires: this is a report at two removes. Dymock, an English compiler, is quoting the Makhzan-el-Adwiya (a Persian/Unani materia medica) summ", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H248';  -- Chirata / Chiretta (Swertia ch
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "唐慎微 Tang Shenwei, 證類本草 (Zhenglei Bencao / Classified Materia Medica), Siku Quanshu recension", "year": "1108", "excerpt": "連翹味苦平無毒主寒熱鼠瘻瘰癧癰腫惡瘡癭瘤結熱蠱毒去白蟲一名異翹一名蘭華一名折根一名軹一名三㢘生太山山谷八月採隂乾\n\n[same entry, within the annotation:] 臣禹錫等謹按蜀本云連翹微寒", "locator": "卷十一 (juan 11), 草部下品之上, 連翹條. Text retrieved whole from zh.wikisource.org page 證類本草 (四庫全書本)/卷11 via action=raw; the block carries the internal marker <子部,醫家類,證類本草,卷十一>.", "assigns": "TWO VALUES SIT IN THE ONE ENTRY AND I AM NOT RESOLVING THEM. (a) 平 = neutral/level, in the Bencao Jing line that Zhenglei carries as its head text. (b) 微寒 = slightly cold, i.e. COOL, attributed in the same entry''s annotation to the 蜀本 (Shu Bencao): 「蜀本云連翹微寒」. If the row stores ''Cool'', (b) matches an", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H177';  -- Forsythia (Lian Qiao) — Forsyt
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard / Thomas Johnson, The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes (Johnson''s revised and enlarged edition)", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "The Temperature. The leaves and berries of this thorn are cold and dry in the second degree: and as Galen also affirmeth, they are of thin parts, and have a certain cutting quality. The Virtues. A. The leaves are used of divers to season meat with, and instead of a salad, as be those of Sorrel. B. The decoction thereof is good against hot burnings and choleric agues: it allayeth the heat of the blood, and tempereth the overmuch heat of the liver.", "locator": "The Third Book, Chap. 23, ''Of Barberries'', under ''The Temperature''. Text file used: https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt, lines 69675-69775 (Temperature paragraph at lines 69745-69749). The same chapter''s Virtues, item G, records the row''s part: ''The bark of the roots is also used in medicines for the jaundice, and that with good success.''", "assigns": "Cold and dry in the second degree — but assigned expressly to THE LEAVES AND BERRIES, not to the root bark. The stored temperature for this row was not supplied in my brief, so I cannot state match-or-differ; I report the source value as-is.", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H188';  -- Barberry (Berberis vulgaris)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Culpeper, Nicholas, The Complete Herbal — simples commentary, ''ROOTS'', s.v. Bistortæ; corroborated by the same book''s ''A Catalogue of Simples in the New Dispensatory'', ROOTS degree lists, and by the main herbal entry ''Bistort, or Snakeweed''.", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "_Bistortæ, &c._ Of Bistort, or snakeweed, cold and dry in the third degree, binding: half a dram at a time taken inwardly, resists pestilence and poison, helps ruptures and bruises, stays fluxes, vomiting, and immoderate flowing of the menses, helps inflammations and soreness of the mouth, and fastens loose teeth, being bruised and boiled in white wine, and the mouth washed with it.", "locator": "Culpeper, The Complete Herbal, simples commentary, section ''ROOTS'', entry ''Bistortæ, &c.'' (Gutenberg pg49513 full text, file line 15354). Independent confirmation in the same book, ''A Catalogue of Simples in the New Dispensatory'', ROOTS: ''Cold in the third degree. Bistort and Mandrakes are cold in the third degree, and Henbane in the fourth.'' (file line 18283) and ''Dry in the third degree. Angelic", "assigns": "Cold and dry in the third degree. MATCHES the stored temperature = Cold and moisture = Dry, and the same passages independently carry the stored Astringent (''binding''; ''of a hard astringent taste''; the second sort ''of an austere binding taste''). This row can be closed on Culpeper alone.", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H213';  -- Bistort (Persicaria bistorta)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal / The English Physician and Family Dispensatory", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "Absinthium, &c. Wormwood. Its several sorts, are all hot and dry in the second or third degrees, the common Wormwood is thought to be hottest, they all help weakness of the stomach, cleanse choler, kill worms, open stoppings, help surfeits, clear the sight, resist poison, cleanse the blood, and secure cloaths from moths.", "locator": "Culpeper, ''The English Physician and Family Dispensatory'', catalogue of simples, section ''HERBS AND THEIR LEAVES'', s.v. Absinthium. Verified in Project Gutenberg e-text of The Complete Herbal, pg49513 (https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt), lines 16133-16137. Corroborated twice more in the same volume: the degree lists in ''A CATALOGUE OF SIMPLES'' place ''Common and Roman Wormwood''", "assigns": "HOT and dry, second or third degree, with common wormwood (= A. absinthium) named as the hottest of the sorts. Culpeper''s own degree list narrows this to hot in the 2nd degree / dry in the 2nd degree; Gerard 1633 gives hot in the 2nd, dry in the 3rd. The direction is unambiguous and consistent acros", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H115';  -- Wormwood (Artemisia absinthium
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Dymock, W., Warden, C. J. H. & Hooper, D., Pharmacographia Indica: A History of the Principal Drugs of Vegetable Origin, Met With in British India, Vol. II", "year": "1891", "excerpt": "History, Uses, &C. — These plants, in Sanskrit Chitraka, are described as digestive, light, astringent, hot and appetizing; a remedy for dyspepsia, piles, leprosy, anasarca, worms, cough, phlegm, wind and biliousness. In the Nighantas, among other synonyms, they bear the names Daruna, Dahana, and Agni, in allusion to their burning and acrid properties.", "locator": "Vol. II, p. 329, order PLUMBAGINEAE. The entry heading \"PLUMBAGO ZEYLANICA, Linn.\" stands on p. 328 and \"PLUMBAGO ROSEA, Linn.\" on p. 329; the vernaculars and the \"History, Uses, &c.\" paragraph on p. 329 are shared by both species. Verified in archive.org b21960343_0002 (title page VOL. II ... 1891); the volume''s own contents list gives \"Plumbago zeylanica ... 328 / ,, rosea ... 329\".", "assigns": "HOT (ushna virya, reported), reinforced in the next sentence by the nighantu synonyms Daruna, Dahana and Agni \"in allusion to their burning and acrid properties\". NOTE ON MATCH: the stored value was not supplied to me. The audit note already records this passage as supporting HOT, and I confirm it v", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H300';  -- Chitrak (Ceylon Leadwort) — Pl
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard / Thomas Johnson, The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes (Johnson''s revised and enlarged edition)", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "The Temperature and virtues. ... D. The branches, leaves, and tender buds of the Olive tree do cool, dry, and bind, and especially of the wild Olive; for they be of greater force than those of the tame: therefore by reason they be milder they are better for eye medicines, which have need of binding things to be mixed with them. E. The same do stay St. Anthony''s fire, the shingles, epinyctides, night wheals, carbuncles, and eating ulcers.", "locator": "The Third Book, Chap. 56, ''Of the Olive Tree'', under the combined heading ''The Temperature and virtues'', paragraph D. Text file used: https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt, lines 73675-73752 (paragraph D at lines 73745-73750).", "assigns": "''cool, dry, and bind'' — cool on the heat axis, dry on the moisture axis, plus astringency. MATCHES the stored row on BOTH axes (stored temperature ''Cool'', stored moisture ''Dry'', per the project''s own citation_excerpt_audit.json record for H207), and it matches the part_used exactly: the assignment i", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H207';  -- Olive Leaf (Olea europaea)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "William Dymock, C. J. H. Warden and David Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica: A History of the Principal Drugs of Vegetable Origin Met With in British India", "year": "1890-1893", "excerpt": "The small cardamom, in Sanskrit Ela, is mentioned by Susruta. In the Nighantas it bears various synonyms, such as Truti, Kapota-varni \"grey,\" Korangi, and Dravidi \"coming from the Dravidian country.\" The large or Nepal cardamom (Amomum subulatum) is called Sthulaila \"large Ela,\" and is described separately. Both kinds are considered to be digestive, pungent, light and hot, and are recommended in phlegmatic affections, such as cough, asthma, piles, and diseases of the bladder and kidneys.", "locator": "Vol. III, Order Scitamineae, entry ''ELETTARIA CARDAMOMUM, Maton'', History, Uses, &c., p. 428. Verified in the full OCR of archive.org b21960343_0003 (page header ''428 SCITAMINEAE'' immediately precedes the entry heading; the next header is ''429'').", "assigns": "HOT (ushna virya), reported by Dymock from the Sanskrit nighantus for Ela: ''digestive, pungent, light and hot''. This is a THERMAL DIRECTION MATCH but not a literal match: the row''s stored value is Warm, and this source says hot. I am not resolving that; reporting both. Note also the second, independ", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H128';  -- Cardamom (Ela) — Elettaria car
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Stewart, J. Lindsay, Punjab Plants, comprising botanical and vernacular names, and uses of most of the trees, shrubs, and herbs of economical value growing within the Province — N.O. Convolvulaceae, ''C. PLURICAULIS. Chois.'', p. 150. Quoted verbatim and independently in Watt, George, A Dictionary of the Economic Products of India, Vol. II (1889), s.v. Convolvulus pluricaulis, p. 519.", "year": "1869", "excerpt": "Watt (1889), Vol. II, p. 519, quoting Stewart: \"Food and Fodder.—\" It is eaten by cattle and is reckoned cooling, and used as a vegetable or given in sherbet\" (Stewart).\"  //  Stewart (1869), p. 150, same sentence in the original, as it stands in the archive.org OCR: \"C. PLU&iCAULis. Chois. Vernacular, porpr^mgy gorahh pdHw, baphalH, dddak. A common plant in. many places throughout the Punjali plains. It is eaten by cattle^ and is. reckoned- cooling, and used as a vegetable or given in sharbcU.\" (OCR noise in the Stewart scan is transcribed as-is; the Watt quotation above is the clean witness to the same sentence.)", "locator": "Stewart, Punjab Plants (1869), N.O. CONVOLVULACEAE, p. 150, entry ''C. PLURICAULIS. Chois.'' — verified in archive.org identifier punjabplantscom00stewgoog, file punjabplantscom00stewgoog_djvu.txt, file lines 9486-9491 (page 151 running head appears at file line ~9515, confirming the entry sits on p. 150; Stewart''s own index also directs ''Convolvulus pluricaulis, 150''). Watt, Dictionary of the Econo", "assigns": "Cooling (''is reckoned cooling''). The stored temperature for this row was not legible in the truncated audit note I was given, so I cannot state match/difference — a stored Cool or Cold would MATCH. IMPORTANT QUALIFICATION for the founder: this is an explicit thermal word applied to the plant itself,", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H238';  -- Shankhpushpi (Convolvulus plur
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, Pharmacopœia Londinensis / The London Dispensatory, entry \"Granatorum. Of Pomegranates\" and the appended \"Catalogue of Simples\" degree lists (FRUITS and BARKS)", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "[main entry] \"_Granatorum._ Of Pomegranates. The rind cools, and forcibly binds, stays fluxes, and the menses, helps digestion, strengthens weak stomachs, fastens the teeth, and are good for such whose gums waste. You may take a dram of it at a time inwardly. Pomegranate flowers are of the same virtue.\"\n\n[Catalogue of Simples, FRUITS] \"_Cold in the first degree._ The flesh of Citrons, Quinces, Pears, Prunes, &c.\n\n_In the second._ Gourds, Cucumbers, Melons, Pompions, Oranges, Lemons, Citrons, Pomegranates, viz. the juice of them, Peaches, Prunes, Galls, Apples.\"\n\n[Catalogue of Simples, BARKS] \"_Cold in the first._ Oak, Pomegranates.\"\n\n[corroboration, Gerard 1633, Chap. 96 \"Of the Pomegranate Tree\", The Temperature and Virtues] \"C. The sour ones, and especially if they be withal something harsh, do evidently cool, dry, and something bind.\"", "locator": "Culpeper, main entry \"Granatorum\" (Gutenberg #49513, line 16007); Catalogue of Simples, FRUITS section (header line 19023), degree list lines 19063–19067; BARKS section (header line 18427), degree list line 18464. Gerard 1633, Book 3, Chap. 96, \"The Temperature and Virtues\", paragraphs C, F and H (herball.txt, chapter head line 77295).", "assigns": "COLD. Culpeper assigns it by part: the FRUIT/JUICE is \"cold in the second\" degree; the BARK/rind is \"cold in the first\" degree; and his main entry says the rind \"cools, and forcibly binds\". Gerard says the sour fruit and seeds \"evidently cool, dry, and something bind\" and that the flowers are \"like ", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H144';  -- Pomegranate (Punica granatum)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Udoy Chand Dutt, The Materia Medica of the Hindus, Compiled from Sanskrit Medical Works (Calcutta: Thacker, Spink & Co.)", "year": "1877", "excerpt": "TERMINALIA ARJUNA, Bedd. Sans. Arjuna. Kukubha. Vern. Arjuna, Beng. Kahu, Hind. THE bark of this timber tree is considered tonic, astringent and cooling, and is used in heart diseases, contusions, fractures, ulcers, etc. In diseases of the heart it is used in a variety of ways. Thus a decoction of the bark with milk is given as a nourishment.", "locator": "Part II, Nat. Order Combretaceae, entry ''TERMINALIA ARJUNA, Bedd.'', p. 163 (entry runs pp. 163-164). Verified against the archive.org OCR of the 1877 printing, item materiamedicaofh00duttuoft, file materiamedicaofh00duttuoft_djvu.txt; the running head ''TERMINALIA ARJUNA. 163'' immediately precedes the entry and ''164 SANSKRIT MATERIA MEDICA'' immediately follows it.", "assigns": "''cooling'' — an explicit thermal quality, Dutt''s English rendering of the classical sita virya. MATCHES the stored value as described in the herb note (temperature ''cooling''). Note the taste string ''astringent'' and the cardiovascular indication sit in the same sentence, so this one line carries taste", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H148';  -- Arjuna — Terminalia arjuna
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal — the appended ''A Catalogue of Simples in the New Dispensatory''", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "Helxine. Pellitory of the wall. Cold, moist, cleansing, helps the stone and gravel in the kidnies, difficulty of urine, sore throats, pains in the ears, the juice being dropped in them; outwardly it helps the shingles and St. Anthony''s fire.", "locator": "A CATALOGUE OF SIMPLES IN THE NEW DISPENSATORY, section ''HERBS AND THEIR LEAVES'', alphabetical entry s.v. ''Helxine'' (between ''Herniaria'' and ''Hyppoglossum''). Project Gutenberg ebook #49513, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt, lines 16770-16773. Corroborating degree lists in the same catalogue: ''Herbs cold in the first degree. Sorrel, Wood-sorrel, Arach, Burdock, Shepherd''s-purs", "assigns": "Cold and moist — and, from the degree lists, cold in the first degree and moist in the first degree. MATCHES the stored ''Cool'' + ''Moist'' on both axes. The catalogue entry also independently carries the row''s stone/gravel and urinary indications, which were previously resting on Grieve (1931).", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H232';  -- Pellitory-of-the-Wall (Parieta
