update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "李時珍 Li Shizhen, 本草綱目 Bencao Gangmu — 卷十八下, 草部·蔓草類, 釣藤 (Diao teng / Gou teng), 氣味 line", "year": "1596", "excerpt": "釣藤{别錄下品}　　校正{自木部移入此} … 氣味甘微寒無毒{保昇曰苦權曰甘平時珍曰初微甘後微苦平} 主治小兒寒熱十二驚癎{别錄}小兒驚啼瘛瘲熱壅客忤胎風{權}", "locator": "本草綱目 (四庫全書本), 卷十八下, 草之七 蔓草類, entry 釣藤 (marked 别錄下品, i.e. carried over from the Mingyi Bielu, lower grade; the Gangmu notes it was moved in from the 木部). Retrieved as raw wikitext from https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/卷18下&action=raw — note the trap held: the bare 卷18 page is a table of contents only (it lists 釣藤 under 蔓草類七十三種), and the entry itself is in 卷18下. The drug is w", "assigns": "甘，微寒，無毒 — sweet, SLIGHTLY COLD, non-toxic. Dissenting sub-notes inside the same 氣味 line: 韓保昇 gives the taste as bitter; 甄權 gives 甘平 (sweet, neutral); 李時珍 himself gives 初微甘後微苦平 (at first slightly sweet, then slightly bitter, neutral). So the head-line assignment is 微寒 (slightly cold / cool) and two o", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H273';  -- Cat's Claw Vine / Uncaria (Gou
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard (rev. Thomas Johnson), The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes, Book 2, Chap. 274 ''Of Cowslips''", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "The Temperature.\n\nThe Cowslips and Primroses are in temperature dry, and a little hot.", "locator": "Gerard, The Herball (1633), Book 2, Chap. 274, ''Of Cowslips'', section ''The Temperature'' (exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt, line 39986; chapter heading at line 39870, Book 2 begins at line 13029). Corroborating: Culpeper, The Complete Herbal, ''A Catalogue of Simples in the New Dispensatory'', HERBS — ''_Intemperate and hot in the first degree, are_ Agrimony, Marsh-mallows, Goose-grass or Cleavers, D", "assigns": "Gerard: ''dry, and a little hot''. Culpeper''s catalogue independently: hot in the first degree AND dry in the first degree. Both MATCH the row''s stored Warm and stored Dry.", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H135';  -- Cowslip (Primula veris)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "William Dymock, C. J. H. Warden and David Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica: A History of the Principal Drugs of Vegetable Origin met with in British India, Vol. III (Part VI)", "year": "1893", "excerpt": "They are called in Sanskrit Tamra-valli (P. urinaria) and Bhumy-amali (P. Niruri), and bear among other synonyms those of Tamalika, Bhu-dhatri, and Bahu-pattra, \"having many leaves.\" Hindu physicians consider them to be deobstruent, diuretic, astringent and cooling, and prescribe the dried plant in powder or decoction in jaundice.", "locator": "Pharmacographia Indica, Vol. III (Part VI), 1893, p. 266, entry PHYLLANTHUS NIRURI, Linn. / PHYLLANTHUS URINARIA, Linn., under ''History, Uses, &c.'' Verified in archive.org item b21960343_0003, file b21960343_0003_djvu.txt, lines 15451-15457; the running head ''266 EUPHORBIACEAE'' opens at line 15437 and ''267'' at line 15491, so the quoted sentence sits on p. 266. Title page confirms Vol. III / Part V", "assigns": "COOLING, stated as an explicit property of the drug alongside deobstruent, diuretic and astringent. This MATCHES the stored COOLING value that the forbidden Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India was being cited for. LABELLING REQUIRED: Dymock is an English compiler reporting the Hindu (Ayurvedic) assignm", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H199';  -- Bhumyamalaki / Chanca Piedra (
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "William Dymock, C. J. H. Warden and David Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica: A History of the Principal Drugs of Vegetable Origin Met With in British India, Vol. II", "year": "1890", "excerpt": "\"The bark is one of the most important articles in the Hindu Materia Medica, and is described in the Nighantés as bitter, astringent, cold and digestive; a remedy for piles, dysentery, bile, leprosy and phlegmatic humours. Susruta says it is expectorant, an antidote to poisons, cures dysuria, urinary and skin diseases, checks nausea and vomiting, removes pruritus, improves the condition of bad ulcers, relieves pains of the stomach…\"", "locator": "Pharmacographia Indica, Vol. II, p. 392, entry \"HOLARRHENA ANTIDYSENTERICA, Wall.\" (Apocynaceae), under \"History, Uses, &c.\" — the passage sits between the running heads \"392 APOCYNACEÆ\" and \"APOCYNACEÆ 393\". Scan: archive.org mobot31753000263373, mobot31753000263373_djvu.txt lines 19782–19835.", "assigns": "COLD (\"bitter, astringent, cold and digestive\"). This MATCHES the stored Cool/cold (Sheeta virya) and in the same sentence also matches the stored Bitter (Tikta) and Astringent (Kashaya), plus the headline dysentery indication. All three energetics and the primary indication come out of one clause.", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H223';  -- Kutaj (Kurchi) — Holarrhena an
