update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "李時珍 Li Shizhen, 本草綱目 Bencao Gangmu, 卷三十二 果部·果之四 味類, 「茗」(即茶) entry, 葉·氣味 line", "year": "1596", "excerpt": "葉氣味苦甘㣲寒無毒", "locator": "本草綱目 (四庫全書本) 卷32, 果之四 味類十三種, entry 「茗」{{唐本草}}, the 葉 (leaf) 氣味 line. Retrieved from https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/卷32&action=raw (52,788 bytes; the 茗 entry begins at line 110 of the raw wikitext, the 氣味 line is line 113). 卷32 has no 上/下 split, so the bare page is the full text, not a table of contents.", "assigns": "㣲寒 = slightly cold. MATCHES the row''s stored ''Cool (bordering Cold)'' precisely. The same line also gives 苦 (bitter) and 甘 (sweet), matching the row''s ''Bitter … mildly Sweet'' and their order.", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H160';  -- Green Tea (Camellia sinensis)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard (rev. Thomas Johnson), The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "The Temperature.\n\nThe seed of Mustard, especially that which we chiefly use, doth heat and make thin, and also draweth forth. It is hot and dry in the fourth degree, according to Galen.", "locator": "Book 2, Chap. 9. Of Mustard — section \"The Temperature\" (immediately following \"The Names\", preceding \"The Virtues\" para. A). Full text downloaded from https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt (modernised-spelling transcription of the 1633 edition), lines 13795-13812.", "assigns": "Hot and dry in the FOURTH degree (Galenic). MATCHES the stored value ''Hot'', and additionally carries the stored moisture ''Dry''.", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H116';  -- Black Mustard (Brassica nigra)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, Pharmacopœia Londinensis / The London Dispensatory, entry \"Lentiscus. Mastich-tree\" and the appended \"Catalogue of Simples\" (Gums and Rosins degree list)", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "[main entry] \"_Lentiscus._ Mastich-tree. Both the leaves and bark of it stop fluxes (being hot and dry in the second degree) spitting and evacuations of blood, and the falling out of the fundament.\"\n\n[Catalogue of Simples, 2. Of the Gums and Rosins] \"_Gums are either temperate_, _as_, Lacca, Elemi, Tragacanth, &c.\n\n_Intemperate_, _and so are hot in the first degree_, as Bdellium, Gum of Ivy.\n\n_In the second_, Galbanum, Myrrh, Mastich, Frankincense, Olibanum, Pitch, Rozin, Styrax.\"\n\n[DIVERGENT WITNESS, Gerard 1633, Chap. 84 \"Of the Lentisk, or Mastic Tree\", The Temperature] \"The leaves, bark, and gum of the Mastic tree are of a mean and temperate heat, and are dry in the second degree, and somewhat astringent.\"", "locator": "Culpeper, main entry \"Lentiscus\" (Gutenberg #49513, line 16879); Catalogue of Simples, \"2. Of the Gums and Rosins\", degree list lines 19392–19398; further Culpeper virtues of the gum at lines 19580–19586. Gerard 1633, Book 3, Chap. 84, \"The Temperature\" (herball.txt, chapter head line 76083, Temperature paragraph line ~76155).", "assigns": "HOT AND DRY IN THE SECOND DEGREE (Culpeper), for both the leaves/bark and, in the Gums list, for the gum Mastich itself. SOURCES DISAGREE AND I AM NOT RESOLVING IT: Gerard 1633 gives the same drug as \"of a mean and temperate HEAT, and are dry in the second degree\" — i.e. Gerard makes it thermally TE", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H298';  -- Mastic (Chios mastic gum) — Pi
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "William Dymock, C. J. H. Warden and David Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica: A History of the Principal Drugs of Vegetable Origin Met With in British India, Vol. III", "year": "1893", "excerpt": "The fruit of this tree is the Dhatriphala, Amritaphala, Amalaka or Srijihala of the Nighantas, and is described as having all the properties of the chebulic myrobalan. It is used both fresh and dried; in the former condition it is considered to be refrigerant, diuretic and laxative; in the latter, astringent. […] Mahometan physicians esteem this myrobalan equally with the Hindus; they describe it as astringent, refrigerant, cardiacal, and a purifier of the humors of the body. It is much prescribed by them in fluxes, and is also applied externally on account of its cooling and astringent properties.", "locator": "Vol. III, entry ''PHYLLANTHUS EMBLICA, Linn.'', section ''History, Uses, &c.'', pp. 261-262 (entry runs pp. 261-264; the refrigerant/cooling sentences are on p. 262). Verified first-hand from archive.org item b21960343_0003, file b21960343_0003_djvu.txt, downloaded to my own directory; the printed page numbers 260 and 261 are legible in the OCR immediately above the entry heading. OCR note: the runnin", "assigns": "Cold / cooling. Dymock''s own words are ''refrigerant'' (twice) and ''cooling''. This MATCHES the stored value ''Cool''. SECOND-HAND LABEL, as required: Dymock is an English compiler reporting what the Nighantas and the Hindu and Muslim physicians hold, not the classical text itself; the sentence is explic", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H125';  -- Amalaki (Amla / Indian Goosebe
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "李時珍 Li Shizhen, 本草綱目 Bencao Gangmu", "year": "1596", "excerpt": "根氣味苦平無毒〔别録曰大寒……元素曰氣凉味苦甘氣厚味薄浮而升陽中隂也〕\n\n〔發明〕時珍曰……蓋黄芩氣寒味苦色黄帶緑苦入心寒勝熱瀉心火治脾之濕熱", "locator": "本草綱目 (四庫全書本) 卷十三 草部之二 山草類下, 黄芩 (本經中品) — 「根氣味」 field and the 「發明」 field. Raw wikisource text: https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/卷13&action=raw , lines 73-77 (黄芩 heading at line 73, 氣味 at line 76, 發明 at line 77). Note the bare 卷13 page is NOT a table of contents here — it carries the full text.", "assigns": "The source speaks with two voices and I am not resolving them. (a) The 氣味 HEADLINE reads 苦、平 (bitter, NEUTRAL) — carried over verbatim from the 本經. (b) Inside that same 氣味 field BCGM records 別錄曰大寒 (Mingyi Bielu: GREATLY COLD) and 元素曰氣凉 (Zhang Yuansu: qi is COOL). (c) In 發明, Li Shizhen states in his ", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H142';  -- Baikal Skullcap (Huang Qin) — 
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard, The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes, enlarged and amended by Thomas Johnson", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "The Temperature.\n\nThese certainly, especially the two first, are cold, and that in the later end of the third or beginning of the fourth degree (if opium be in the fourth.)", "locator": "Book 2, Chap. 38, \"Of Wild Lettuce\", section \"The Temperature\". Text consulted: the exclassics.com transcription of the 1633 edition (https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt), which carries chapter numbers but not the original page numbers, so no page is asserted.", "assigns": "COLD, in the late third or early fourth Galenic degree. Cold as a property of the drug, not an action word. The stored temperature for this row is not stated in my brief, so I cannot say match or differ; I can only say the source assigns COLD to a high degree. Salmon 1710 independently assigns cold ", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H119';  -- Wild Lettuce (Lactuca virosa)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard, rev. Thomas Johnson, The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "The Temperature. Butterbur is hot and dry in the second degree, and of thin parts. The Virtues. A. The roots of Butterbur stamped with ale, and given to drink in pestilent and burning fevers, mightily cooleth and abateth the heat thereof. B. The roots dried and beaten to powder, and drunk in wine, is a sovereign medicine against the plague and pestilent fevers, because it provoketh sweat, and driveth from the heart all venom and ill heat", "locator": "Chap. 292, ''Of Butterbur'', under the heading ''The Temperature''. Verified against the exclassics transcription of the 1633 Johnson revision, https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt. The chapter covers ONE plant only (Petasites; figs. 1204 Butterbur in Flower and 1205 The Leaves of Butterbur), so the shared-chapter trap does not apply here — I checked the whole chapter from the Description thr", "assigns": "''hot and dry in the second degree''. MATCHES the stored values on both axes: temperature Warm and moisture Dry. Independently corroborated by Culpeper 1653, Catalogue of Simples, roots section: ''Petacitæ. Of Butter-bur. The roots are hot and dry in the second degree, they are exceeding good in violen", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H226';  -- Butterbur
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, Pharmacopoeia Londinensis / The Complete Herbal — \"A Catalogue of Simples in the New Dispensatory,\" section on Gums, Rosins, Balsams and Juices made thick", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "_Myrrh_ is hot and dry in the second degree, dangerous for pregnant women, it is bitter, and yet held to be good for the roughness of the throat and wind-pipe; half a dram of it taken at a time helps rheumatic distillations upon the lungs, pains in the sides; it stops fluxes, provokes the menses, brings away both birth and after-birth, softens the hardness of the womb\n\n[corroborating degree-list in the same Catalogue, a few pages earlier:]\n_Gums are either temperate_, _as_, Lacca, Elemi, Tragacanth, &c.\n_Intemperate_, _and so are hot in the first degree_, as Bdellium, Gum of Ivy.\n_In the second_, Galbanum, Myrrh, Mastich, Frankincense, Olibanum, Pitch, Rozin, Styrax.", "locator": "Culpeper, Catalogue of Simples in the New Dispensatory, \"Gums, Rozins, Balsams, and Juices made thick,\" entry \"Myrrh\"; the degree-list for gums stands in the same section a few entries earlier. Verified against the full Project Gutenberg text of Culpeper''s Complete Herbal, ebook #49513 (https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt), lines 19517-19525 (main entry) and 19393-19398 (degree ", "assigns": "Hot and dry in the second degree. This MATCHES the stored value of Warm, and does so as an explicit Galenic degree rather than by inference from an action word. Culpeper states the degree twice independently (in the running entry and in the tabulated degree-list for gums), so the reading does not de", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H109';  -- Myrrh (Commiphora myrrha), ole
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Udoy Chand Dutt, The Materia Medica of the Hindus, compiled from Sanskrit medical works, Calcutta, 1877", "year": "1877", "excerpt": "PIPER LONGUM, Linn.\n\nSyn. Chavica Roxburghii, Miq.\n\nSans. Pippali. Kana, Krishna.\nVern. Pipul, Beng. Pipal, Hind.\n\nTHE dried catkins and the root of Piper longum are used in medicine. They are considered heating, stimulant, carminative, alterative, laxative and useful in cough, hoarseness, asthma, dyspepsia, paralysis, etc.", "locator": "pp. 243-244, entry \"PIPER LONGUM, Linn.\" (archive.org materiamedicaofh00duttuoft, _djvu.txt lines 10307-10320; printed page number 243 visible in the running head)", "assigns": "\"heating\" — an explicit thermal quality, listed first in the properties line, ahead of the action words. This is Dutt reporting the Sanskrit works, i.e. ushna virya rendered into English. It MATCHES a warm/hot stored value. Gerard 1633 corroborates independently from the Galenic side with a degree: ", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H158';  -- Pippali / Long Pepper (Piper l
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "李時珍《本草綱目》 (Li Shizhen, Bencao Gangmu), 四庫全書本", "year": "1596", "excerpt": "莖葉氣味苦辛寒無毒", "locator": "《本草綱目》(四庫全書本) 卷十五, 草部・隰草類, 夏枯草條, 「莖葉・氣味」項. Verified against https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/卷15&action=raw", "assigns": "寒 (cold), with 苦辛 (bitter, acrid) as the taste. This SUPPORTS the stored ''Cool'', though the source''s word is the stronger 寒 (cold) rather than 涼 (cool). BUT SEE THE CONFLICT BELOW — the Western corpus assigns the opposite.", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H123';  -- Selfheal (Prunella vulgaris)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard, revised and enlarged by Thomas Johnson, The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes — Book 2, Chap. 396, \"Of Parsley\"", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "The Temperature.\n\nGarden Parsley is hot and dry, but the seed is more hot and dry, which is hot in the second degree, and dry almost in the third: the root is also of a moderate heat.\n\n[corroborating, from Culpeper 1653, Catalogue of Simples:]\n_Roots hot in the first degree._ Marsh-mallows, Bazil, Valerian, Spattling, Poppy, Burdocks, Borrage, Bugloss, Calamus Aromaticus, Avens, Pilewort, China, Self-heal, Liquorice, Dog-grass, white Lilies, Peony, male and female, wild Parsnips, Parsley, Valerian, great and small, Knee-holly, Satyrion, Scorzonera, Skirrets.", "locator": "Gerard/Johnson 1633, Book 2, Chap. 396 \"Of Parsley,\" heading \"The Temperature\" (the paragraph immediately preceding \"The Virtues\" A-D). Verified in the complete ex-classics transcription of the 1633 edition (https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt), lines 53023-53026; chapter heading at line 52965, Book 2 begins at line 13029. Corroborating Culpeper 1653: Catalogue of Simples, \"Temperature o", "assigns": "Gerard: hot and dry, with the seed hot in the second degree and dry almost in the third, and the root \"of a moderate heat.\" Culpeper''s degree-tables give a lower reading for the root (hot in the first degree, dry in the second) and a higher one for the seed (hot in the third, dry in the third). Both", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H155';  -- Parsley (Petroselinum crispum)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "William Dymock, C. J. H. Warden and David Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica: A History of the Principal Drugs of Vegetable Origin Met With in British India, Vol. I, BERBERIDEAE — BERBERIS ARISTATA, DC. (with B. Lycium and B. asiatica)", "year": "1890", "excerpt": "In the bazaars the stem, extract and fruit are always obtainable; the two first are considered cold and dry, and are prescribed in combination with other bitters and aromatics, as tonics and antiperiodics, especially when bilious symptoms and diarrhoea are present; they are also used in menorrhagia.", "locator": "Dymock, Warden & Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica, Vol. I (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner; Bombay: Education Society''s Press; Calcutta: Thacker, Spink, 1890), p. 66, in the History, Uses, &c. section of the joint entry headed BERBERIS ARISTATA, DC. (entry opens p. 64; the quoted sentence falls between the p. 65 and p. 67 page marks). Archive.org b21960343_0001, b21960343_0001_djvu.txt, lines 43", "assigns": "COLD AND DRY, assigned to the stem/wood (Dāruharidrā) and to the extract (Rasānjana/rasot). This MATCHES the row''s stored ''Cool''. Separately on the same page the FRUIT is called ''cooling and acid'' — a different part, and not the part the row uses.", "corpus": "unani", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H288';  -- Daruharidra / Indian Barberry 
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "汪昂 Wang Ang, 本草備要 Ben Cao Bei Yao", "year": "1694", "excerpt": "白芍药\n补血，泻肝，涩，敛阴\n\n苦酸微寒，入肝脾血分，为手、足太阴（肺脾）行经药。", "locator": "本草備要, 草部 (herb section), entry 白芍藥. Text consulted: Chinese Wikisource transcription (zh.wikisource.org, title 本草備要, action=raw), which is set in SIMPLIFIED characters; the excerpt is verbatim to that transcription, not to a Qing woodblock.", "assigns": "微寒 - SLIGHTLY COLD (with tastes 苦酸, bitter and sour). This MATCHES the stored temperature Cool, and it also supports the stored leading taste Sour, which the Shennong citation the row currently uses does not.", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H173';  -- White Peony (Bai Shao) — Paeon
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard and Thomas Johnson, The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes, enlarged edition (primary); Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal / Pharmacopoeia Londinensis (independent second witness)", "year": "1633 (Gerard/Johnson); 1653 (Culpeper)", "excerpt": "GERARD 1633: \"The Temperature. Southernwood is hot and dry in the end of the third degree: it hath also force to distribute and to rarify.\" — CULPEPER 1653: \"A Brotanum, mas, fœmina. Southernwood, male and female. It is hot and dry in the third degree, resists poison, kills worms; outwardly in plaisters, it dissolves cold swellings, and helps the bitings of venomous beasts, makes hair grow: take not above half a dram at a time in powder.\"", "locator": "GERARD: Book/Chapter — CHAP. 454, ''Of Southernwoood'' [sic, the chapter heading is misspelt in the source], section ''The Temperature''. Verified first-hand from the complete text at https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt, downloaded to my own directory. CULPEPER: the appended catalogue of simples, section ''HERBS AND THEIR LEAVES'', entry ''A Brotanum, mas, fœmina.'' — this is the CATALOGUE, sepa", "assigns": "HOT and DRY in the third degree (Gerard: ''in the END of the third degree''). Direction MATCHES the stored value ''Warm''; INTENSITY DIFFERS — the end of the third degree is near the top of the Galenic heat scale and reads as ''Hot'' rather than ''Warm''. I am reporting that gap, not resolving it. Note both", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H233';  -- Southernwood (Artemisia abrota
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "趙學敏 Zhao Xuemin, 本草綱目拾遺 (Bencao Gangmu Shiyi, \"Supplement to the Compendium of Materia Medica\"), compiled 1765", "year": "1765", "excerpt": "《百草鏡》云：黨參，一名黃參，黃潤者良，出山西潞安太原等處。有白色者，總以淨軟壯實味甜者佳。嫩而小枝者，名上黨參。老而大者，名防黨參。\n\n味甘性平，治肺虛，能益肺氣。", "locator": "卷三 草部上, entry 上黨參 (zh.wikisource.org, 本草綱目拾遺/卷三, action=raw); the sub-entry 防風黨參 immediately following repeats the assignment: 「味甘平，補中益氣，和脾胃，除煩恼，解渴。中氣微虛，用以調補，甚爲平安。」", "assigns": "性平 / 甘平 — NEUTRAL (平), one of the four qi (寒涼平溫熱), stated twice in the same entry and a third time in the 《本經逢原》 (Zhang Lu, 1695) quotation that opens it: 「雖無甘溫峻補之功，卻有甘平清肺之力。不似沙參之性寒，專洩肺氣也。」 (\"it lacks the sweet-warm drastic tonifying power, but has a sweet-neutral lung-clearing power; unlike sha she", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H215';  -- Codonopsis / Dang Shen (Codono
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal (English Physician Enlarged, with the catalogue of simples)", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "_Marrubium album_, is common Horehound. Hot in the second degree, and dry in the third, opens the liver and spleen, cleanses the breast and lungs, helps old coughs, pains in the sides, ptisicks, or ulceration of the lungs, it provokes the menses, eases hard labour in child-bearing, brings away the placenta.", "locator": "Culpeper, The Complete Herbal (1653), catalogue of simples, section ''Herbs and Their Leaves'', entry ''Marrubium, album, nigrum, foetidum'' (sub-entry Marrubium album). Verified in the full Project Gutenberg text of ebook 49513 (https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt), line 16956. Independent corroboration: Gerard, The Herball (1633), Book 3, Chap. 231 ''Of Horehound'', section ''The Tem", "assigns": "Hot in the second degree (and dry in the third). MATCHES the stored ''Warm'' in direction, and gives it a Galenic degree the stored value does not carry. Note the source also assigns DRY in the third degree, which the row should be checked against.", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H114';  -- White Horehound (Marrubium vul
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal — appended Catalogue of Simples", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "_Seeds are hot in the first degree._\n\nLinseed, Fenugreek, Coriander, Rice, Gromwell, Lupines.\n\n_In the second._ Dill, Smallage, Orobus, Rocket, Bazil, Nettles.\n\n_In the third._ Bishop''s Weed, Annis, Amomus, Carraway, Fennel, (and so I believe Smallage too, let authors say what they will, for if the herb of Smallage be somewhat hotter than Parsley; I know little reason why the seed should not be so hot) Cardamoms, Parsley, Cummin, Carrots, Nigella, Navew, Hartwort, Staves Ager.\n\n[and, in the same catalogue] _Dry in the... third._ Dill, Smallages, Bishop''s Weed, Annis, Caraway, Cummin, Coriander, Nigella, Gromwell, Parsley.", "locator": "Catalogue of Simples, section SEEDS, the hot/cold and moist/dry degree lists that follow the College roster. Gutenberg ebook #49513, pg49513.txt: hot-degree lists at lines 19198-19210, dry-in-the-third list at lines 19225-19226.", "assigns": "HOT in the SECOND degree, with Culpeper arguing in his own voice for the THIRD; and DRY in the third degree. MATCHES the stored ''Warm'' and also carries the stored moisture ''Dry''.", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H247';  -- Celery Seed (Apium graveolens)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "William Dymock, C. J. H. Warden and David Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica: A History of the Principal Drugs of Vegetable Origin Met With in British India", "year": "1893", "excerpt": "Nutmegs, in Sanskrit Jati and Jatiphala, are mentioned by Susruta, and in the Nighantas bear various synonyms, such as Jati-kosha, Jati-sara, Shaluka, and Majja-sara ; they are considered to be hot, digestive, carminative, expectorant and anthelmintic. Mace is called Jati-pattri, and is said to have similar properties.", "locator": "Vol. III, order Myristiceae, entry \"MYRISTICA FRAGRANS, Houtt.\", section \"History, Uses, &c.\", p. 192 (running head 192 verified immediately above the entry; the entry runs on to pp. 193-194). Text consulted: archive.org b21960343_0003_djvu.txt. Diacritics in the excerpt are as printed; the OCR renders them inconsistently (e.g. \"Jdti-kosha\", \"Maj ja-sara\") and I have restored only the obvious OCR ", "assigns": "HOT (ushna virya), reported for both nutmeg and mace. This is Dymock at second hand: he is an English compiler stating what the Nighantus and Susruta hold, and it must be labelled as a report of the classical texts rather than as the classical text itself. The stored temperature is not stated in my ", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H224';  -- Nutmeg & Mace (Jatiphala) - My
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "李時珍 Li Shizhen, 本草綱目 Bencao Gangmu (Siku Quanshu recension)", "year": "1596", "excerpt": "花氣味辛温無毒", "locator": "卷十五 (juan 15), 草之四 隰草類, entry 紅藍花 (釋名 紅花), the 氣味 line for the flower. Verified first-hand: I fetched https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/卷15&action=raw into my own directory (170,277 bytes — the full juan text, not a table of contents; the bare 卷15 page is complete for this juan, no 上/下 split exists here). The entry sits between 雞冠 and 畨紅花 in the running text. Wikisource mark", "assigns": "辛温 — pungent and WARM, 無毒 non-toxic. This MATCHES the stored value ''Warm'', and it also supplies the row''s stored taste ''Pungent'' (辛) from the same four characters. The commentary appended in the same entry doubles the reading twice over: 元素曰…謂其苦温 (Zhang Yuansu: bitter and warm) and 好古曰辛而甘苦温 (Wang Ha", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H260';  -- Safflower (Hong Hua) — Cartham
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard, revised and enlarged by Thomas Johnson, The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes — Book 3, Chap. 17, \"Of Broom, and Broomrape\"", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "The Temperature and Virtues.\n\nA. The twigs, flowers, and seeds of Broom are hot and dry in the second degree: they are also of a thin essence, and are of force to cleanse and open, and especially the seed, which is dryer and not so full of superfluous moisture\n\n[corroborating, from Culpeper 1653, Catalogue of Simples, Herbs:]\n_Genista._ Brooms: hot and dry in the second degree, cleanse and open the stomach, break the stone in the reins and bladder, help the green sickness. Let such as are troubled with heart-qualms or faintings, forbear it, for it weakens the heart and spirit vital.", "locator": "Gerard/Johnson 1633, Book 3, Chap. 17 \"Of Broom, and Broomrape,\" heading \"The Temperature and Virtues,\" paragraph A. Verified in the complete ex-classics transcription (https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt), lines 69080-69084; chapter heading at line 68956, Book 3 begins at line 66324. Corroborating Culpeper 1653: Catalogue of Simples, Herbs, entry \"Genista. Brooms\" (Gutenberg #49513, lin", "assigns": "Hot and dry in the second degree, from two independent Galenic witnesses that agree exactly. This supports Warm. NOTE: I was not told the row''s stored value (the failure note is truncated at \"Does NOT support temperature or...\"), so I cannot state whether this MATCHES or DIFFERS — please check it ag", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H237';  -- Broom / Scotch Broom (Cytisus 
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The English Physician and Family Dispensatory (Catalogue of Simples, \"Of the Gums and Rosins\" — degree list for Concrete Juices), first issued 1649-1653", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "_Concrete Juices, or Juices made thick, are either_\n\n_Temperate_, _as_, Juice of Liquorice, white starch.\n\n_Hot in the first degree._ Sugar.\n\n_In the second._ Labdanum.\n\n_In the third._ Benzoin, Assafœtida.", "locator": "Catalogue of Simples, after the College list of \"Gums, Rozins, Balsams, and Juices made thick\", under Culpeper''s own heading \"1. Of the Juices. 2. Of the Gums and Rosins\" (lines 19318-19333 of Project Gutenberg #49513, culpeper1653.txt)", "assigns": "HOT IN THE THIRD DEGREE — an explicit Galenic degree. The list runs Temperate / Hot in the first degree (Sugar) / In the second (Labdanum) / In the third (Benzoin, Assafœtida), so the elided head of the last line is \"Hot\". Two allowed non-Western witnesses agree on hot and neither gives a degree: Dy", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H240';  -- Asafoetida (Devil's Dung, Hing
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "李時珍 Li Shizhen, 本草綱目 Bencao Gangmu", "year": "1596", "excerpt": "補骨脂〔宋開寳〕……〔氣味〕辛，大温，無毒。〔權曰苦辛。珣曰惡甘草。時珍曰忌芸薹及諸血，得胡桃胡麻良。〕主治五勞七傷，風虚冷，骨髓傷敗，腎冷精流……", "locator": "本草綱目 (四庫全書本) 卷十四 草部之三 芳草類, 補骨脂 (宋開寳) — the 「氣味」 field. Raw wikisource: https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/卷14&action=raw , entry heading at line 211, 氣味 line at line 214.", "assigns": "辛，大溫 — acrid, GREATLY WARM. MATCHES the stored ''Warm'' (and supports the row''s ''very warm to hot'' gloss). Unambiguous, single-voiced, no dissent recorded inside the field.", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H296';  -- Bakuchi (Babchi) — Psoralea co
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "神農本草經 Shennong Bencao Jing, 孫星衍 Sun Xingyan recension", "year": "1799", "excerpt": "地榆\n\n味苦，微寒。主婦人乳□痛，七傷、帶下病，止痛，除惡肉，止汗，療金創。生山谷。", "locator": "神農本草經 (孫星衍輯), 中品, 草部, entry 地榆. Text consulted: zh.wikisource.org, title 神農本草經_(孫星衍), action=raw. One character in 主婦人乳□痛 is illegible in that transcription and is marked □; I have not guessed it.", "assigns": "微寒 - SLIGHTLY COLD, with taste 苦 (bitter). Direction MATCHES the stored temperature Cold; the degree is slightly softer (\"slightly cold\" rather than flatly cold). It also supports the bitter element of the stored Bitter/Sour/Astringent taste.", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H251';  -- Great Burnet (Sanguisorba offi
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal (originally The English Physitian Enlarged), chapter ''Juniper Bush'', Government and virtues", "year": "1653", "excerpt": "The berries are hot in the third degree, and dry but in the first, being a most admirable counter-poison, and as great a resister of the pestilence, as any growing; they are excellent good against the biting of venomous beasts, they provoke urine exceedingly, and therefore are very available to dysuries and stranguaries. It is so powerful a remedy against the dropsy, that the very lye made of the ashes of the herb being drank, cures the disease.", "locator": "Culpeper, The Complete Herbal, s.v. ''JUNIPER BUSH'', section ''Government and virtues''. Verified in the full Project Gutenberg text of ebook 49513 (https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt), which I downloaded with curl; the chapter heading is at file line 7286 and the quoted sentence at lines 7301-7304. The same degree is repeated independently in the appended catalogue of simples, se", "assigns": "Hot in the third degree, dry in the first. MATCHES the stored value ''Warm'' in direction, but note the source is more emphatic than ''Warm'': third degree is the second-hottest Galenic rank. Gerard 1633 agrees on the degree but is slightly softer on the fruit specifically (''the berries are also hot but", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H106';  -- Juniper Berry (Juniperus commu
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Li Shizhen 李時珍, Bencao Gangmu 本草綱目, 木部 (Wood section), 木之二 喬木類 (Tall Trees), entry 杜仲 (Du Zhong), 【氣味】 (Qi and Flavour) heading", "year": "1596", "excerpt": "【氣味】\n\n辛，平，無毒。\n\n《別錄》曰︰甘，溫。權曰︰苦，暖。元素曰：性溫，味辛、甘。\n\n[and, from 時珍''s 【發明】 in the same entry:] 杜仲色紫而潤，味甘微辛，其氣溫平。甘溫能補，微辛能潤。", "locator": "Bencao Gangmu, 木部, 木之二 （喬木類五十二種）, entry 杜仲, under the 【氣味】 heading; the 時珍 line is under 【發明】 in the same entry. Conventionally this section is juan 35 (卷三十五), but the edition I read carries no juan number on the page, so I am citing by section and entry rather than asserting a juan. Verified in the full Chinese Wikisource text of 本草綱目/木之二 fetched with action=raw (https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index", "assigns": "溫 (warm) and 暖 (warm), qualified by 平 (neutral). Li Shizhen stacks the tradition rather than flattening it: the Shennong Bencao Jing reading he heads with is 辛，平 (pungent, neutral), then 《別錄》 gives 甘，溫 (sweet, warm), 甄權 gives 苦，暖 (bitter, warm), and 張元素 gives 性溫 (warm in nature). Li Shizhen''s own su", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H272';  -- Eucommia Bark (Du Zhong)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Gerard, John (rev. Thomas Johnson), The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes, London, 1633 — Chap. 443, \"Of Gith, or Nigella\"", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "The Temperature.\n\nThe seed of the garden Nigella is hot and dry in the third degree, and of thin parts.", "locator": "Gerard 1633, Book 2, Chap. 443 \"Of Gith, or Nigella\", section \"The Temperature\" (immediately following \"The Names\"). Verified in the Ex-Classics full transcription of the 1633 Johnson edition, https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt, lines 57091-57094. Note: the Ex-Classics edition modernises spelling; the substance of the Temperature clause is unaltered.", "assigns": "Hot and dry in the THIRD degree (Galenic). Corroborated independently by Salmon, Botanologia (1710), Chap. 410 \"Of Katherine Flower, or Roman Nigella\", §IX The Qualities: \"The Seed, (which is chiefly used) is hot and dry in the third degree ... where note, the first Kind is only used in Physick\" — h", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H112';  -- Black Cumin / Nigella / Kalonj
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard and Thomas Johnson, The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes, Chap. 531 \"Of Rue, or Herb-Grace\", section \"The Temperature\"", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "Rue is hot and dry in the later end of the third degree; and wild Rue in the fourth: it is of thin and and subtle parts: it wastes and consumes wind, it cutteth and digesteth gross and tough humours.", "locator": "Gerard/Johnson, The Herball (1633), Book 2, Chap. 531, \"Of Rue, or Herb-Grace\", under the heading \"The Temperature\". Transcription: https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt (line 66211). Cross-checked verbatim against the original-spelling scan, archive.org b30322674_0001, _djvu.txt line 137496: \"Ruc is hot and dry in the later end of the third degree, and wilde Rue in the fourth: it is of th", "assigns": "HOT AND DRY IN THE (LATER END OF THE) THIRD DEGREE. The chapter covers two plants, so I checked the multi-plant trap explicitly: Gerard''s Temperature sentence itself splits them, assigning the third degree to Rue (Ruta hortensis / garden Rue = R. graveolens, named as such in the Names section) and t", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H117';  -- Rue (Ruta graveolens)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Gerard, John (rev. Thomas Johnson), The Herbal, or General History of Plants — Chap. 90, ''Of True Saffron, and the wild or Spring Saffrons'', section ''The Temperature''. Corroborated by Culpeper, The Complete Herbal, ''A Catalogue of Simples in the New Dispensatory'', FLOWERS.", "year": "1633", "excerpt": "Saffron is a little astringent or binding, but his hot quality doth so over-rule in it, that in the whole essence it is in the number of those herbs which are hot in the second degree, and dry in the first: therefore it also hath a certain force to concoct, which is furthered by the small astriction that is in it, as Galen saith.", "locator": "Gerard/Johnson 1633, Chap. 90 ''Of True Saffron, and the wild or Spring Saffrons'', under ''The Temperature'' (immediately following ''The Names'', which identifies the plant as Crocus / Saffaran / Zahafaran). Verified against the Ex-Classics full text of the 1633 Johnson edition, https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt (file lines 9434-9438). Culpeper corroboration: The Complete Herbal, ''A Catalo", "assigns": "Hot in the second degree, dry in the first degree. MATCHES the stored temperature = Warm and moisture = Dry. Two independent Western witnesses give the identical degree pair (Gerard 1633 and Culpeper 1653), so this is an unusually firm result.", "corpus": "galenic-european", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H110';  -- Saffron / Kesar (Crocus sativu
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Dymock, Warden & Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica: A History of the Principal Drugs of Vegetable Origin Met With in British India, vol. III — \"Plantago ovata, Forsk.\"", "year": "1893", "excerpt": "In India, they are considered to be cooling and demulcent, and useful in inflammatory and bilious derangements of the digestive organs. The crushed seeds made into a poultice with vinegar and oil are applied to rheumatic and gouty swellings. With the mucilage a cooling lotion for the head is made.", "locator": "Pharmacographia Indica, vol. III, Plantagineae, entry \"PLANTAGO OVATA, Forsk.\" (syn. P. Ispaghula), \"History, Uses, &c.\", p. 126. Verified in archive.org item mobot31753000263381 (Missouri Botanical Garden copy, v. 3), file mobot31753000263381_djvu.txt, lines 6690-6712; the printed running head \"126 PLANTAGINEAE\" stands immediately above the entry and \"PLANTAGINEAE 127\" below it. The set was issue", "assigns": "COOLING (Indian/Unani sita). Reported by Dymock as the Indian practitioners'' assignment, not as his own Western-pharmacological judgement — this is an English compiler reporting the tradition at second hand, and must be labelled that way. I do not know the value stored in the row (the herbs.md brief", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H159';  -- Psyllium (Plantago ovata)
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "William Dymock, C. J. H. Warden and David Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica, Vol. II (reporting the Nighantas)", "year": "1890", "excerpt": "It is called in Sanskrit Kesaraja, Bhringaraja and Markava, names which include Wedelia calendulacea, which is regarded by the natives of India as a variety of Eclipta alba. In the Nighantas it is described as bitter, pungent, hot, and dry, removing phlegm and wind, increasing the appetite, and curing diseases of the skin, eye and head.", "locator": "Pharmacographia Indica, Vol. II, Compositae, ''Eclipta alba, Hassk.'', p. 266 (entry heading and quoted sentence both fall between the page markers 266 and 267). Verified in archive.org b21960343_0002_djvu.txt, lines 14553-14572; cross-checked against mobot31753000263373_djvu.txt line 13044 (same text, poorer OCR).", "assigns": "Hot (with dry), reported from the Nighantas. Whether this MATCHES the row could not be determined because the stored temperature was not given in my task file. See the divergence flagged below before accepting it.", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "tier": "second-hand-report", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H198';  -- Bhringaraj / False Daisy (Ecli
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "吳儀洛 Wu Yiluo, 本草從新 Ben Cao Cong Xin, 卷三 草部, 甘菊花", "year": "1757", "excerpt": "甘菊花　宣、祛風熱、補肺腎、明目。甘苦微寒。備受四氣。（冬苗、春葉、夏蕊、秋花。）饱經霜露。得金水之精。能益肺腎二臟。以制心火而平肝木。木平則風息。火降則熱除。故能養目血。去翳膜。…治目淚頭眩。散溼痹游風。", "locator": "本草從新 (1757), 卷三 草部, entry 甘菊花. Text: Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=本草從新&action=raw (retrieved 2026-07-22).", "assigns": "微寒 (wei han) = SLIGHTLY COLD, i.e. Cool. THE PRE-1900 CHINESE CORPUS IS NOT UNANIMOUS AND I AM NOT RESOLVING IT. 神農本草經 (Sun Xingyan recension, 1799), 鞠華: 「味苦，平。」 = bitter, NEUTRAL. 本草綱目 (1596), 卷十五, 菊, headline 氣味: 「氣味苦平無毒」 = bitter, NEUTRAL, non-toxic - and Li Shizhen''s 發明 reinforces it, 「味兼甘苦性禀平和」", "corpus": "chinese", "tier": "first-hand", "agreement": "agrees with stored value"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H169';  -- Chrysanthemum (Ju Hua) - Chrys
-- ── 8 disagreements: RECORDED ONLY. No stored temperature is changed. ──────
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard and Thomas Johnson, The Herball, or Generall Historie of Plantes, Chap. 383 \"Of Septfoil, or Tormentil\", section \"The Temperature\"", "year": "1633", "assigns": "DRY IN THE THIRD DEGREE, with \"very little heat\" - i.e. strongly drying and only faintly warm. THE TWO GALENIC WITNESSES DISAGREE ON THE HEAT AND I AM NOT RESOLVING IT. Culpeper, Catalogue of Simples, \"Roots\", s.v. Tormentillae: \"Of Tormentil. A kind of Sinqfoil; dry in the third degree, but moderat", "agreement": "DISAGREES with the stored value. RECORDED, NOT APPLIED, per the standing rule that classical sources corroborate, disambiguate or add but never override a clear modern value.", "stored_pole": "cool", "source_pole": "hot"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H222';  -- Tormentil (Potentilla erecta DISAGREEMENT
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Culpeper, Nicholas, The Complete Herbal — simples commentary, ''SEEDS OR GRAINS'', s.v. Annis; and ''A Catalogue of Simples in the New Dispensatory'', SEEDS degree lists. Corroborated (with a different degree) by Gerard/Johnson, The Herbal (1633), Chap. 414 ''Of Anise'', ''The Temperature''.", "year": "1653", "assigns": "HOT and DRY. Culpeper: hot in the third degree, dry in the third degree. Gerard: reports Galen at hot and dry in the third degree, then concludes for himself ''hot in the second, dry in the first''. The two allowed sources agree on the QUALITY (hot / dry) and disagree on the DEGREE; per the working ag", "agreement": "DISAGREES with the stored value. RECORDED, NOT APPLIED, per the standing rule that classical sources corroborate, disambiguate or add but never override a clear modern value.", "stored_pole": "warm", "source_pole": "neutral"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H194';  -- Anise (Aniseed) — Pimpinella DISAGREEMENT
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Pomet, Pierre, A Compleat History of Druggs (English translation of Histoire Generale des Drogues), London, 1712 — Book VII, chap. 14, \"Of the Cacao, or Chocolate-Nut\"", "year": "1712", "assigns": "COOLING (explicitly thermal, not merely a taste claim — though Pomet argues to it from the taste). I do not know the value stored in the row, so I cannot state MATCH or DIFFER. IMPORTANT: the pre-1900 sources DISAGREE on this drug and I am not resolving the disagreement. See notes.", "agreement": "DISAGREES with the stored value. RECORDED, NOT APPLIED, per the standing rule that classical sources corroborate, disambiguate or add but never override a clear modern value.", "stored_pole": "warm", "source_pole": "cold"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H241';  -- Cacao (Cocoa) DISAGREEMENT
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, Pharmacopœia Londinensis / The London Dispensatory, \"A Catalogue of Simples\" (appended degree lists), author Nicholas Culpeper", "year": "1653", "assigns": "COLD IN THE SECOND DEGREE, applied specifically to the SEED (Culpeper''s SEEDS degree list). Culpeper separately gives the fruit as \"cold and moist\"; Gerard gives the whole plant as \"of a cold nature, with plenty of moisture.\" All three agree on COLD + MOIST. The stored temperature value was not supp", "agreement": "DISAGREES with the stored value. RECORDED, NOT APPLIED, per the standing rule that classical sources corroborate, disambiguate or add but never override a clear modern value.", "stored_pole": "neutral", "source_pole": "cold"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H118';  -- Pumpkin Seed (Cucurbita pepo DISAGREEMENT
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal — ''A Catalogue of Simples in the New Dispensatory'', section SEEDS OR GRAINS (degree lists)", "year": "1653", "assigns": "HOT IN THE FIRST DEGREE. Both allowed Western witnesses agree on the degree. NOTE ON MOISTURE, reported not resolved: the row stores moisture = Moist, but Gerard (reporting Galen) puts linseed ''in a mean between moist and dry'', i.e. temperate in moisture, not moist; Culpeper''s catalogue does not lis", "agreement": "DISAGREES with the stored value. RECORDED, NOT APPLIED, per the standing rule that classical sources corroborate, disambiguate or add but never override a clear modern value.", "stored_pole": "cool", "source_pole": "hot"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H113';  -- Flax / Linseed (Linum usitat DISAGREEMENT
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "Li Shizhen 李時珍, Bencao Gangmu 本草綱目 (Compendium of Materia Medica)", "year": "1596", "assigns": "苦平微寒 — bitter; neutral, slightly COLD. This is an explicit thermal assignment (微寒). Whether it MATCHES the stored value depends on the stored value, which I was not given; 微寒 ''slightly cold'' sits at the cool end and would support Cool, and would NOT support Warm or Neutral-only. Within the same 氣味 l", "agreement": "DISAGREES with the stored value. RECORDED, NOT APPLIED, per the standing rule that classical sources corroborate, disambiguate or add but never override a clear modern value.", "stored_pole": "cool", "source_pole": "neutral"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H267';  -- Capillaris Wormwood (Yin Che DISAGREEMENT
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard, The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes, enlarged and amended by Thomas Johnson", "year": "1633", "assigns": "HOT, at the end of the second Galenic degree (dry in the first-to-second). The stored temperature is not stated in my brief; the source supports a warm/hot assignment and would contradict any cool or neutral value.", "agreement": "DISAGREES with the stored value. RECORDED, NOT APPLIED, per the standing rule that classical sources corroborate, disambiguate or add but never override a clear modern value.", "stored_pole": "warm", "source_pole": "neutral"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H146';  -- Dill (Ayurvedic Shatapushpa; DISAGREEMENT
update public.herbs set energetics_citation = '{"source": "John Gerard, The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes (Thomas Johnson revised edition), Book 3, CHAP. 10 ''Of Senna'', section ''The Temperature''", "year": "1633", "assigns": "''Of a mean temperature, neither hot nor cold, yet inclining to heat, and dry almost in the third degree.'' This DIFFERS from, and directly contradicts, the stored value ''Cold''. Culpeper 1653 is harsher still and also contradicts it: ''It heats in the second degree and dries in the first.'' Both allowed", "agreement": "DISAGREES with the stored value. RECORDED, NOT APPLIED, per the standing rule that classical sources corroborate, disambiguate or add but never override a clear modern value.", "stored_pole": "cold", "source_pole": "neutral"}'::jsonb where herb_id = 'H127';  -- Senna (Senna alexandrina) DISAGREEMENT
commit;
